<p>Thailand’s former Prime Minister Thaksin Shinawatra was released on parole from Klong Prem Central Prison in Bangkok on Monday after serving eight months of a one-year sentence linked to corruption and abuse-of-power cases. The 76-year-old telecommunications billionaire must wear an electronic monitor during a four-month probation period, Thai corrections authorities said, according to reports. Thaksin left the prison in a white shirt and blue pants, embraced family members including his daughter Paetongtarn Shinawatra, and greeted supporters outside the Bangkok prison. His release follows a Supreme Court ruling in September 2025 that he had wrongly stayed in hospital after returning from exile and must serve jail time; he returned to Thailand in 2023 after about 15 years abroad and had an eight-year sentence later reduced to one year by royal commutation. Thaksin governed from 2001 until a 2006 military coup and built the political network that became Pheu Thai, which has long drawn rural support and opposed Thailand’s pro-military and pro-royalty establishment. His release came after Pheu Thai recorded its weakest election performance on record earlier this year, while Paetongtarn was removed as prime minister by a court order in August 2025.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/bxjzYfoPNlR_4NX7xIoSvvTTh3kJEw7ELGvNjUuq-TtzSDD7sZYf5SKRMmr2BRb4BiU2UGqRoMhQHwuHt92uvKJ2hqtOxW2RGlsTdMbjwQ' alt='Thaksin Shinawatra reacts after being released on parole at Klong Prem Central Prison in Bangkok, Thailand.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Morning logistics: Thai PBS reported that police and corrections officials set up barriers and a designated media area outside Klong Prem, with security arrangements planned through midday.</li><li>Parole basis: Thai media reported that parole approval cited Thaksin’s age, health conditions, past contributions, good behavior and completion of two-thirds of his sentence.</li><li>Special-treatment dispute: After his 2023 return, Thaksin stayed in a private hospital room while serving time, prompting speculation that he had benefited from special treatment as his party returned to power.</li><li>Family network: Four Thai prime ministers have belonged to the Shinawatra political dynasty, including Thaksin, his sister Yingluck and his daughter Paetongtarn.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Thai Department of Corrections: The department said Thaksin’s parole followed legal procedures and that officials had found no disciplinary breach during his imprisonment. (<a href='https://www.thaipbs.or.th/news/content/505738'>Thai PBS</a>)</li><li>Paetongtarn Shinawatra: Paetongtarn said after a final prison visit that she had not discussed politics with her father and had spoken only about family matters. (<a href='https://www.letemps.ch/articles/condamne-pour-corruption-l-ancien-premier-ministre-thailandais-thaksin-shinawatra-va-sortir-de-prison'>Le Temps</a>)</li><li>New York Times: The paper reported on what Thaksin might do next and said he still held influence through Pheu Thai’s role in the governing coalition. (<a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/05/10/world/asia/thailand-thaksin-shinawatra-released-prison.html'>The New York Times</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/asia/southeast-asia/article/3353074/thailands-former-prime-minister-thaksin-released-parole'>Thailand’s Thaksin steps out of jail into landscape where old rivals hold sway</a> - scmp.com</li><li><a href='https://abcnews.com/International/wireStory/thailands-thaksin-released-prison-after-serving-8-months-132835995'>Thailand&#x27;s Thaksin released from prison after serving 8 months for abuse of power</a> - abcnews.com</li><li><a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/thailands-former-pm-thaksin-released-from-prison/article70964407.ece'>Thailand&#x27;s former PM Thaksin released from prison</a> - thehindu.com</li><li><a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c99l1590kr0o'>Thailand&#x27